[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...Bd6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/800f596d-c08d-44ef-ac28-8a4712bc35d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. This move usually leads to various ultra-theoretical Sicilians such as the Najdorf or the Dragon, but we have a way to avoid these lines. Instead of the typical d4, we are going to develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {The main way for Black to proceed is to develop their kingside knight. It's played 40% of the time, in almost 5 million games in the database. The idea is to get us to advance our d-pawn and transpose back into normal lines. But we'll advance our e-pawn! This is an extremely rare and dangerous approach.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. Now our knight is active in the center and covering some key squares in Black's position.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {This chapter will deal with 5...e6, the most common continuation. This is played 30% of the time. Black is simply preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop. Against ...e6, we'll go for g3. Our plan here is to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Bd6 {Our move of interest in this study is the natural ...Bd6, developing the bishop and attacking our knight. This is played half the time. Against this, we'll move our knight back to c4 and target Black's bishop.} 7.Nc4 7...Be7 {Black's second option is to slide the bishop back to the e7-square. This is played 28% of the time. We'll fianchetto, preparing to castle.} 8.Bg2 8...O-O {Black usually castles their king. We'll also castle.} 9.O-O 9...Nc6 {Black develops their other knight. I suggest we go d3, preparing to develop our bishop to e3 or f4. Trading on c6 was also possible but I really like the g2-bishop.} 10.d3 10...a6 {Black goes ...a6, controlling the b5 square. Ideally, Black wants to play ...Bd7 and advance their b-pawn, but we won't allow them to achieve that.} 11.a4 {We push our a-pawn, controlling b5 and looking to advance a5 in order to freeze Black's queenside. Our position is great and we'll look to proceed by developing our dark-squared bishop to either e3 or f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...Bd6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/800f596d-c08d-44ef-ac28-8a4712bc35d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. This move usually leads to various ultra-theoretical Sicilians such as the Najdorf or the Dragon, but we have a way to avoid these lines. Instead of the typical d4, we are going to develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {The main way for Black to proceed is to develop their kingside knight. It's played 40% of the time, in almost 5 million games in the database. The idea is to get us to advance our d-pawn and transpose back into normal lines. But we'll advance our e-pawn! This is an extremely rare and dangerous approach.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. Now our knight is active in the center and covering some key squares in Black's position.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {This chapter will deal with 5...e6, the most common continuation. This is played 30% of the time. Black is simply preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop. Against ...e6, we'll go for g3. Our plan here is to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Bd6 {Our move of interest in this study is the natural ...Bd6, developing the bishop and attacking our knight. This is played half the time. Against this, we'll move our knight back to c4 and target Black's bishop.} 7.Nc4 7...Bc7 {The most common way to proceed is to slide the bishop back to c7. This is played 50% of the time. Now we'll fianchetto our bishop. This bishop is really annoying for Black since it prevents them from easily developing the light-squared bishop or advancing the b-pawn.} 8.Bg2 8...O-O {Black usually castles their king. We'll also castle.} 9.O-O 9...Nc6 {Black develops the b8-knight.} 10.b3 {And now we go b3, preparing to develop our dark-squared bishop. We've got a powerful light-squared bishop and a pleasant position that's relatively easy to play. Our c1-bishop can develop to either b2 or a3. It's also often useful to push the a-pawn in order to further stop Black from ever going ...b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...Bd6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/800f596d-c08d-44ef-ac28-8a4712bc35d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. This move usually leads to various ultra-theoretical Sicilians such as the Najdorf or the Dragon, but we have a way to avoid these lines. Instead of the typical d4, we are going to develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {The main way for Black to proceed is to develop their kingside knight. It's played 40% of the time, in almost 5 million games in the database. The idea is to get us to advance our d-pawn and transpose back into normal lines. But we'll advance our e-pawn! This is an extremely rare and dangerous approach.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. Now our knight is active in the center and covering some key squares in Black's position.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {This chapter will deal with 5...e6, the most common continuation. This is played 30% of the time. Black is simply preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop. Against ...e6, we'll go for g3. Our plan here is to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Bd6 {Our move of interest in this study is the natural ...Bd6, developing the bishop and attacking our knight. This is played half the time. Against this, we'll move our knight back to c4 and target Black's bishop.} 7.Nc4 7...O-O {Black can also castle and give us the option to capture the bishop. This is played 15% of the time.} 8.Bg2 {Trading our well placed knight for Black's bishop isn't all that great. Instead, I suggest that we proceed by fianchettoing our bishop. We'll castle our king and can then either fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, or develop it via d3 and Be3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...Be7 or 6...Nbd7 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/731aacf2-2509-4976-b196-ef3bf55f9fab/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. Now we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black proceeds in the most natural way by developing the g8-knight. And now we'll push our e-pawn! This rare pawn advance is the signature idea of our Anti-Sicilian.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. This pawn trade has allowed us to get an active knight in the center.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {Black's most common continuation is ...e6, opening up the f8-bishop. This happens 30% of the time. We'll proceed with g3, preparing to fianchetto and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {This study will deal with another option on move six, ...Be7. This is played 25% of the time. The bishop is less active here, but Black's idea is that we can't gain a tempo on the bishop. We'll fianchetto our bishop. As we saw previously, the bishop is a key asset since it makes it difficult for Black to move the b-pawn or develop the c8-bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black usually castles their king. We'll also castle our king.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {The most logical way for Black to proceed is to develop their knight to d7 and propose a knight trade. It wouldn't be great for us to just trade off our active knight, so we'll respond by jumping it back to c4.} 9.Nc4 {And we also castle, transposing into the main line of this study.} 9...Rb8 {The most common way for Black to proceed is ...Rb8, preparing ...b5. This is played half the time. We'll prevent Black from expanding with ...b5 by advancing our a-pawn.} 10.a4 10...b6 {Black goes ...b6, looking to develop their light-squared bishop to b7. We'll go d3, opening up our dark-squared bishop and adding some extra support to the c4-knight.} 11.d3 11...Bb7 {Black develops their light-squared bishop and offers a bishop trade. We'll respond by capturing.} 12.Bxb7 12...Rxb7 {Black recaptures. Now we should bring our queen out with an attack on Black's rook.} 13.Qf3 13...Rb8 {Black moves their rook to safety.} 14.Bf4 {And we develop our dark-squared bishop, attacking the rook again. With dominating piece activity, White is in excellent shape here. We can look to follow up with a5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...Be7 or 6...Nbd7 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/731aacf2-2509-4976-b196-ef3bf55f9fab/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. Now we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black proceeds in the most natural way by developing the g8-knight. And now we'll push our e-pawn! This rare pawn advance is the signature idea of our Anti-Sicilian.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. This pawn trade has allowed us to get an active knight in the center.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {Black's most common continuation is ...e6, opening up the f8-bishop. This happens 30% of the time. We'll proceed with g3, preparing to fianchetto and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {This study will deal with another option on move six, ...Be7. This is played 25% of the time. The bishop is less active here, but Black's idea is that we can't gain a tempo on the bishop. We'll fianchetto our bishop. As we saw previously, the bishop is a key asset since it makes it difficult for Black to move the b-pawn or develop the c8-bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black usually castles their king. We'll also castle our king.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {The most logical way for Black to proceed is to develop their knight to d7 and propose a knight trade. It wouldn't be great for us to just trade off our active knight, so we'll respond by jumping it back to c4.} 9.Nc4 {And we also castle, transposing into the main line of this study.} 9...a6 {Black's third option is ...a6. This is played 16% of the time. Black's plan here is to go ...Rb8 and follow up by advancing ...b5. Against ...a6, we'll push our a-pawn to control the b5-square.} 10.a4 10...Rb8 {Black goes ...Rb8, preparing ...b5. And we should proceed by opening up our dark-squared bishop. It seems like we're allowing Black to get what they want, but we're actually setting a little trap.} 11.d3 11...b5 {Black advances with ...b5. We'll capture the b-pawn.} 12.axb5 12...axb5 {Black recaptures with their pawn. Now we'll jump our knight out to the edge of the board, threatening to fork Black's queen and rook.} 13.Na5 13...Rb6 {Black defends against the fork. We jump our knight into c6, putting pressure on the queen and bishop.} 14.Nc6 14...Qe8 {Black has to go ...Qe8 in order to keep the e7-bishop defended.} 15.Ra8 {And we finish the sequence by bringing our rook down to a8. Black is in trouble here. Our pieces are very active and it's really not easy for Black to make moves. Black's queen is overworked, so the immediate threat here is Rxc8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...Be7 or 6...Nbd7 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/731aacf2-2509-4976-b196-ef3bf55f9fab/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. Now we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black proceeds in the most natural way by developing the g8-knight. And now we'll push our e-pawn! This rare pawn advance is the signature idea of our Anti-Sicilian.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. This pawn trade has allowed us to get an active knight in the center.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {Black's most common continuation is ...e6, opening up the f8-bishop. This happens 30% of the time. We'll proceed with g3, preparing to fianchetto and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {This study will deal with another option on move six, ...Be7. This is played 25% of the time. The bishop is less active here, but Black's idea is that we can't gain a tempo on the bishop. We'll fianchetto our bishop. As we saw previously, the bishop is a key asset since it makes it difficult for Black to move the b-pawn or develop the c8-bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black usually castles their king. We'll also castle our king.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {The most logical way for Black to proceed is to develop their knight to d7 and propose a knight trade. It wouldn't be great for us to just trade off our active knight, so we'll respond by jumping it back to c4.} 9.Nc4 {And we also castle, transposing into the main line of this study.} 9...Nb6 {Black's second option is to insist on trading the knights with ...Nb6. This is played 31% of the time. We should respond with b3, supporting the c4-knight and freeing up the b2-square for our other bishop.} 10.b3 10...Nxc4 {Black captures the c4-knight.} 11.bxc4 {And we recapture. Our pawns are doubled, but they aren't weak and the pawn on c4 covers the important d5-square. Our plan here is to play d3 and develop the dark-squared bishop. We should also look to bring our rook to the semi-open b-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...Be7 or 6...Nbd7 / Variation 3"]
