1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. Now we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black proceeds in the most natural way by developing the g8-knight. And now we'll push our e-pawn! This rare pawn advance is the signature idea of our Anti-Sicilian.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. This pawn trade has allowed us to get an active knight in the center.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {Black's most common continuation is ...e6, opening up the f8-bishop. This happens 30% of the time. We'll proceed with g3, preparing to fianchetto and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {This study will deal with another option on move six, ...Be7. This is played 25% of the time. The bishop is less active here, but Black's idea is that we can't gain a tempo on the bishop. We'll fianchetto our bishop. As we saw previously, the bishop is a key asset since it makes it difficult for Black to move the b-pawn or develop the c8-bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black usually castles their king. We'll also castle our king.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {The most logical way for Black to proceed is to develop their knight to d7 and propose a knight trade. It wouldn't be great for us to just trade off our active knight, so we'll respond by jumping it back to c4.} 9.Nc4 {And we also castle, transposing into the main line of this study.} 9...Nb6 {Black's second option is to insist on trading the knights with ...Nb6. This is played 31% of the time. We should respond with b3, supporting the c4-knight and freeing up the b2-square for our other bishop.} 10.b3 10...Nbd5 {Instead of taking, Black can also jump their knight into the center.} 11.Bb2 {Against this, we can simply fianchetto our other bishop. We've got two powerful bishops and a pretty comfortable position. Our game plan here is to go Qe2, bring the rooks to the center and eventually advance our d-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Part 1 (5...e6) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...Be7 or 6...Nbd7 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/dd5530e2-ba63-4c2e-8231-3697ab680e44/studies/731aacf2-2509-4976-b196-ef3bf55f9fab/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop our kingside knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6, controlling e5 and opening the light-squared bishop. Now we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black proceeds in the most natural way by developing the g8-knight. And now we'll push our e-pawn! This rare pawn advance is the signature idea of our Anti-Sicilian.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. This pawn trade has allowed us to get an active knight in the center.} 5.Nxe5 5...e6 {Black's most common continuation is ...e6, opening up the f8-bishop. This happens 30% of the time. We'll proceed with g3, preparing to fianchetto and castle kingside.} 6.g3 6...Nbd7 {Instead of ...Be7 or ...Bd6, Black can develop their other knight. This, however, quickly transposes. We don't want to trade our active knight, so we'll bring it back to c4.} 7.Nc4 7...Be7 {Black goes ...Be7, preparing ...O-O. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 8.Bg2 8...O-O {Black castles their king.} 9.O-O {And we also castle, transposing into the main line of this study.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...e6 {The most common move at this point is ...e6. Although blocking their light-squared bishop, Black has to develop and castle somehow. We'll give a check on b5. It's a common motif in positions we cover here.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. But we haven't played Bb5+ to trade. We'll play Bg5 and pin the knight. Black is already in trouble.} 10.Bg5 10...Bxb5 {Capturing on b5 might look like a decent attempt, but it doesn't work. We'll recapture with the knight. Now there's a fork threatened on c7. Our queen on e5 is simply a monster.} 11.Nxb5 11...Rc8 {The rook coming to c8 seems like a decent defense. But our activity is just not going to stop. We'll improve another piece with tempo, attacking the queen this time. The point is to dislodge it from protecting the c7- and the d6-squares.} 12.Rd1 12...Qa5+ {The most logical move is Black grabbing the tempo back with a check.} 13.c3 {Blocking with the pawn is sufficient here. Now that the queen has moved, Nd6+ is a great idea for a discovered attack on it. Black has to deal with that. Also, we will look to castle soon, with all of our pieces blooming. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...g6 {7...g6 might seem like a logical move, and it's played 14% of the time. Black wants to develop the bishop on the dangerous diagonal and threaten our pieces that way. However, it's a mistake because it removes one of the defenders of the f6-knight, and we'll take advantage of that by attacking it. Things could get messy for Black soon.} 8.Bg5 8...Bg7 {Sure, they will develop and defend, probably thinking there's no danger. But we'll castle long quickly. This is one of the perks of developing the queen and the dark-squared bishop first.} 9.O-O-O 9...O-O {Black castles as well. We actually have a winning combination now. First, we'll take on d7.} 10.Nxd7 10...Nxd7 {Black has to recapture with the knight. Anything else would lose material. Now our queen is under attack, but we wanted to move it anyway.} 11.Qb4 {We'll slide to b4, and look at all the targets we have in the position. Our rook is pinning the knight on the d-file, and we want to play Bb5 to add an attacker to it. Meanwhile, our queen and our bishop are attacking the e7-pawn and Black can't play ...e6. Too much to deal with.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...e6 {Black can choose to play ...e6 on move 7, and the position will transpose to Study 2. It's played about 25% of the time. We'll activate the bishop on f4, anticipating a possible central trade at some point. We also have ideas of Nc4-Nd6+.} 8.Bf4 8...Bc5 {You might face this attack, but that's fine.} 9.Qc4 {We simply slide to c4 and there's some tension among the pieces. We'll cover this in more depth in the second study, as mentioned.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...a6 {The move ...a6 is only played 5% of the time, and it's an inaccuracy. Black leaves us a lot of space and time to develop and target some weak spots. We'll activate the bishop to f4. It will soon play a role.} 8.Bf4 8...e6 {Black plays ...e6, trying to develop their kingside.} 9.Nc4 {Once they do that, we can bring the knight to c4. From there, it hits the d6-square and we're actually threatening to jump there with check. Black trading the bishop for our knight on d6 would be good for us. White is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...a6 {They could play ...a6 here, covering the b5-square. But our plans don't change much. This move is simply passive. We'll line up the bishop with the queen.} 9.Bf4 9...e6 {Once Black opens up the dark-squared bishop...} 10.Be2 {We'll develop and prepare to castle. Our light-squared bishop is coming to f3 and both bishops will be strong. Since the queen is already out, it's convenient to place the rook on the d1-square and attack the Black queen. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...e6 {The most common move at this point is ...e6. Although blocking their light-squared bishop, Black has to develop and castle somehow. We'll give a check on b5. It's a common motif in positions we cover here.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. But we haven't played Bb5+ to trade. We'll play Bg5 and pin the knight. Black is already in trouble.} 10.Bg5 10...Be7 {Be7 might seem like the best defense, but White is already winning according to the computer. Black's pieces are stuck together and we will continue applying pressure. We'll bring the rook on d1 to pin the bishop to the queen. One of its defenders, the knight on f6, is already under attack.} 11.Rd1 11...O-O {It might even be the best move for the Black king to get out of the way. The pinned bishop does lose one of its defenders, however. Black's idea is that they are threatening to play ...Qa5 now, and pin our bishop back! That's why we will retreat to e3.} 12.Qe3 12...a6 {If our bishop on b5 is attacked now, we can force Black's hand. When the king is castled, it's different. We will capture on f6 first. The point is that Black has to take the bishop on b5, because otherwise the bishop on d7 falls.} 13.Bxf6 13...axb5 {And that's what Black will do. We'll take on e7 now.} 14.Bxe7 14...Qxe7 {The queen recaptures. But we have a plan to win a piece even here.} 15.Nd5 {And it's Nd5! Taking advantage of the pin, we attack the queen and are going to jump to b6 on the next move and fork the bishop and the rook. If Black tries to keep everything together with ...Qe8, then Nc7 is a fork.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 2"]
