1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...e6 {The most common move at this point is ...e6. Although blocking their light-squared bishop, Black has to develop and castle somehow. We'll give a check on b5. It's a common motif in positions we cover here.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. But we haven't played Bb5+ to trade. We'll play Bg5 and pin the knight. Black is already in trouble.} 10.Bg5 10...Be7 {Be7 might seem like the best defense, but White is already winning according to the computer. Black's pieces are stuck together and we will continue applying pressure. We'll bring the rook on d1 to pin the bishop to the queen. One of its defenders, the knight on f6, is already under attack.} 11.Rd1 11...O-O {It might even be the best move for the Black king to get out of the way. The pinned bishop does lose one of its defenders, however. Black's idea is that they are threatening to play ...Qa5 now, and pin our bishop back! That's why we will retreat to e3.} 12.Qe3 12...Nd5 {It might seem like ...Nd5 solves all of Black's problems, but our queen is still on the e-file. Do you see a tactic that wins for White? We'll capture on d5, but with the rook! The rook also protects the bishop on g5.} 13.Rxd5 13...exd5 {So Black captures it. But now the e-file opens. And we can capture on e7, forking the Black rook and queen.} 14.Bxe7 14...d4 {A good attempt to complicate things is ...d4, forking our pieces back. But a simple trade wins. We'll just take the queen.} 15.Bxd8 15...dxe3 {Black captures ours.} 16.Bxd7 {But now the bishop on d7 is hanging. We will come out from this skirmish with two pieces against a rook, which should be winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...e6 {The most common move at this point is ...e6. Although blocking their light-squared bishop, Black has to develop and castle somehow. We'll give a check on b5. It's a common motif in positions we cover here.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. But we haven't played Bb5+ to trade. We'll play Bg5 and pin the knight. Black is already in trouble.} 10.Bg5 10...Be7 {Be7 might seem like the best defense, but White is already winning according to the computer. Black's pieces are stuck together and we will continue applying pressure. We'll bring the rook on d1 to pin the bishop to the queen. One of its defenders, the knight on f6, is already under attack.} 11.Rd1 11...a6 {You should expect to see ...a6 at some point, sooner rather than later. Once Black attacks the bishop, we can capture on d7. This release of tension doesn't mean they are out of the woods.} 12.Bxd7+ 12...Nxd7 {They can recapture with the knight and it seems like some trades are incoming. But our queen on e5 is so powerful that... The knight moving allowed us to capture on g7! Now Black's rook is under attack.} 13.Qxg7 13...Rf8 {And they slide to f8. It's time to trade on e7 now.} 14.Bxe7 14...Qxe7 {Black recaptures.} 15.Qxh7 {And we can take the pawn on h7. We haven't crushed Black yet, but we are up two pawns, which is enough to win. And Black still has to make some moves to consolidate.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...e6 {The most common move at this point is ...e6. Although blocking their light-squared bishop, Black has to develop and castle somehow. We'll give a check on b5. It's a common motif in positions we cover here.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. But we haven't played Bb5+ to trade. We'll play Bg5 and pin the knight. Black is already in trouble.} 10.Bg5 10...Bxb5 {Capturing on b5 might look like a decent attempt, but it doesn't work. We'll recapture with the knight. Now there's a fork threatened on c7. Our queen on e5 is simply a monster.} 11.Nxb5 11...Qa5+ {Black checking on a5 loses on the spot.} 12.Bd2 {We'll simply retreat with a tempo on the queen. Now Black has to worry about that and won't have time to defend against the fork on c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 1: Black Plays 6...exd4 (All Lines) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/1666d990-07a3-4224-9e08-d7451e4813e3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the standard second move.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black responds with ...d6, connecting the chain. And we'll play the Two Knights approach, activating the knight to c3. We don't want to play d4 and go into the main line theory in this course.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops to f6, and we'll unleash our Anti-Sicilian right away. Let's push e5!} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures, but disrupts their pawn chain. We'll take back. Our knight is extremely active and covers some key squares in the Black position.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {This chapter will deal with 5...Nd7. We have already covered 5...e6 in Chapter 1. 5...Nd7 is a way for Black to try and trade off our active knight immediately. It's played 20% of the time, in just over 30,000 games. We won't capture. Instead, I suggest d4! This is a great way to defend the knight and bring another piece to the game quickly.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {You will most likely see Black capture on d4 here. And we'll take back with the queen, defending the knight. We've already got three pieces out and two of them are in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nxe5 {By far the most common move is Black simply capturing the knight on e5, and it's played almost half the time. However, it's not the best move. We'll just recapture and keep our queen active.} 8.Qxe5 8...g6 {This move doesn't work at all. It's a huge blunder that immediately loses for Black. So, look out for this because it seems natural and I expect a lot of players to play it. Now Bb5+ wins, as you will see. Our idea is to pin one of Black's pieces, and then develop and attack its defenders.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black will most likely block with the bishop. There's no point in capturing on d7. Instead, we'll develop to g5 and put pressure on the knight that is defending the bishop. Note that the knight is pinned to the rook for the moment.} 10.Bg5 10...Bg7 {Of course, Black plays ...Bg7 to defend. But their troubles aren't over. Since our bishop has been developed, what can we play now? We can castle queenside! This pins Black's bishop and completely restricts their position.} 11.O-O-O 11...O-O {If they castle, they will simply lose a piece. We'll take on d7.} 12.Bxd7 12...Nxd7 {The knight recaptures, but it's pinned to the queen now.} 13.Qb5 {And we will side step to b5. Black can't defend the knight and is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nxe5 {What if Black decides to make some trades here? This is definitely a variation we have to explore because a lot of players look to trade when they find themselves in unknown theoretical positions. We'll recapture, of course.} 7.dxe5 7...Qxd1+ {A trade of queens will most likely ensue. We'll take back with the king to keep our knight on c3 active. Castling isn't as important here as there isn't much danger to our king.} 8.Kxd1 8...Ng4 {The more aggressive option for Black is 8...Ng4. It looks dangerous because Black is attacking both the e5- and the f2-pawns and threatening a fork. That's why it's important to know the theory here. We'll give a check first.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. And here's a very important move: we'll push e6! This seems completely counter-intuitive, however our e5-pawn was already under attack and now we are threatening the pinned bishop. Also, we are shutting down their dark-squared bishop completely, which will prove to be crucial in one line.} 10.e6 10...fxe6 {Black can definitely capture with the pawn, but now the e-pawns are doubled, isolated and weak. This is much better for us than them being able to capture on e5 with the knight. Let's defend against the fork on f2. It's not in Black's interests to capture on b5 and face more threats.} 11.Ke2 11...O-O-O {A logical move is to castle here. Black activates the a-rook instead of taking our bishop. We'll protect the bishop with a4 now. We would love to open up the a-file for our rook.} 12.a4 12...a6 {Black won't bite. They force us to make a decision instead. We'll take on d7.} 13.Bxd7+ 13...Rxd7 {Once the rook recaptures, here's another point of a4.} 14.a5 {We can play a5 and take aim at the weaknesses in Black's position. We just locked down the queenside so they can't protect the c5-pawn. Apart from that, the b6-square is an outpost for our knight and the e-pawns are still extremely weak. We'll kick out the knight with h3, play Be3 to attack c5, and go Na4 to threaten Nb6+ with a fork.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nxe5 {What if Black decides to make some trades here? This is definitely a variation we have to explore because a lot of players look to trade when they find themselves in unknown theoretical positions. We'll recapture, of course.} 7.dxe5 7...Qxd1+ {A trade of queens will most likely ensue. We'll take back with the king to keep our knight on c3 active. Castling isn't as important here as there isn't much danger to our king.} 8.Kxd1 8...Ng4 {The more aggressive option for Black is 8...Ng4. It looks dangerous because Black is attacking both the e5- and the f2-pawns and threatening a fork. That's why it's important to know the theory here. We'll give a check first.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. And here's a very important move: we'll push e6! This seems completely counter-intuitive, however our e5-pawn was already under attack and now we are threatening the pinned bishop. Also, we are shutting down their dark-squared bishop completely, which will prove to be crucial in one line.} 10.e6 10...Bxb5 {Capturing on b5 is a viable option, but it allows more threats from us. We'll recapture with the knight. We are letting Black fork us on f2, but we are threatening a fork ourselves. That's why we didn't capture on f7 first. We prefer the Black king to be on e8.} 11.Nxb5 11...O-O-O+ {The best move is for Black to castle. This is with check and avoids the fork. We'll slide to e2. Black can capture on e6 now.} 12.Ke2 12...a6 {Black tries to force back our active knight. We'll simply retreat to c3.} 13.Nc3 13...fxe6 {Black now wins a pawn by capturing on e6, albeit the extra pawn resulting is rather sickly. We aren't done venturing with the knight.} 14.Na4 {We'll jump to a4 with the idea of attacking the c5-pawn, as well as jumping to the b6-square if needed. We'll add an attacker on the pawn in the form of Be3. Of course, we can kick out the knight with h3 at some point. Black has probably played in the most resilient way, but White is still slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nxe5 {What if Black decides to make some trades here? This is definitely a variation we have to explore because a lot of players look to trade when they find themselves in unknown theoretical positions. We'll recapture, of course.} 7.dxe5 7...Qxd1+ {A trade of queens will most likely ensue. We'll take back with the king to keep our knight on c3 active. Castling isn't as important here as there isn't much danger to our king.} 8.Kxd1 8...Ng4 {The more aggressive option for Black is 8...Ng4. It looks dangerous because Black is attacking both the e5- and the f2-pawns and threatening a fork. That's why it's important to know the theory here. We'll give a check first.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. And here's a very important move: we'll push e6! This seems completely counter-intuitive, however our e5-pawn was already under attack and now we are threatening the pinned bishop. Also, we are shutting down their dark-squared bishop completely, which will prove to be crucial in one line.} 10.e6 10...Bxb5 {Capturing on b5 is a viable option, but it allows more threats from us. We'll recapture with the knight. We are letting Black fork us on f2, but we are threatening a fork ourselves. That's why we didn't capture on f7 first. We prefer the Black king to be on e8.} 11.Nxb5 11...Nxf2+ {Black can choose to capture on f2. But that's a losing blunder. We'll move to e2 and attack the knight.} 12.Ke2 12...Nxh1 {Black will take the rook, of course. But we also have a fork with Nc7+.} 13.Nc7+ 13...Kd8 {Black slides to d8... And we'll take the rook in the corner.} 14.Nxa8 14...fxe6 {Now Black takes on e6. But the whole reason behind our position being winning is that they can't open up their dark-squared bishop now, while ours is free to roam the board.} 15.Bf4 {This means that our knight will be able to get out, while Black's won't. We will be up a piece and completely winning. Courtesy of 10.e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nxe5 {What if Black decides to make some trades here? This is definitely a variation we have to explore because a lot of players look to trade when they find themselves in unknown theoretical positions. We'll recapture, of course.} 7.dxe5 7...Qxd1+ {A trade of queens will most likely ensue. We'll take back with the king to keep our knight on c3 active. Castling isn't as important here as there isn't much danger to our king.} 8.Kxd1 8...Nd7 {Retreating to d7 is the more passive option, but not a huge mistake. Black is attacking the e5-pawn. We have bigger fish to fry. We'll jump into the center and threaten a fork.} 9.Nd5 9...Kd8 {Black plays the only reasonable defensive move. Let's develop and protect the e5-pawn.} 10.Bf4 10...e6 {Black can even make an inaccuracy easily in this line. 10...e6 seems natural to attack the knight and open up the bishop.} 11.Bg5+ {But we have this check. Black can neither go to e8 because of the fork, nor block with the bishop on e7. They will most likely have to play ...f6 and lose a pawn at some point. White is slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nxe5 {What if Black decides to make some trades here? This is definitely a variation we have to explore because a lot of players look to trade when they find themselves in unknown theoretical positions. We'll recapture, of course.} 7.dxe5 7...Qxd1+ {A trade of queens will most likely ensue. We'll take back with the king to keep our knight on c3 active. Castling isn't as important here as there isn't much danger to our king.} 8.Kxd1 8...Ng4 {The more aggressive option for Black is 8...Ng4. It looks dangerous because Black is attacking both the e5- and the f2-pawns and threatening a fork. That's why it's important to know the theory here. We'll give a check first.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. And here's a very important move: we'll push e6! This seems completely counter-intuitive, however our e5-pawn was already under attack and now we are threatening the pinned bishop. Also, we are shutting down their dark-squared bishop completely, which will prove to be crucial in one line.} 10.e6 10...fxe6 {Black can definitely capture with the pawn, but now the e-pawns are doubled, isolated and weak. This is much better for us than them being able to capture on e5 with the knight. Let's defend against the fork on f2. It's not in Black's interests to capture on b5 and face more threats.} 11.Ke2 11...a6 {They could attack our bishop here. There's no point in retreating now. We'll capture on d7.} 12.Bxd7+ 12...Kxd7 {The king recaptures. And now we will jump to e4. This is a great move because it not only attacks the c5-pawn, but also has a hidden point to it.} 13.Ne4 13...Kc6 {The idea is that once Black defends...} 14.Ng5 {We can jump to g5 and attack Black's weak structure! It's not convenient for Black to move the king back to the d-file. In fact, the best move is probably ...h6, allowing us to capture on e6. White is slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...e6 {They can open up the dark-squared bishop quickly and look to castle. This is played 11% of the time. We'll activate the dark-squared bishop and provide more protection to the knight on e5. It helps to develop it quickly because we will castle queenside in some lines.} 7.Bf4 7...cxd4 {Black can choose to capture on d4 now. This changes things a bit because their dark-squared bishop is already open. We'll recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Bc5 {Black's idea is that they can develop to c5 with tempo on our queen. We'll keep the queen close, though. Continuing to apply pressure is best. Black's pieces are solid for now, but we are aiming to castle queenside.} 9.Qc4 9...O-O {Black castles and gets their king into safety. First of all, we'll capture on d7. This trade is good because it makes our upcoming threat harder to defend.} 10.Nxd7 10...Nxd7 {The knight recaptures. Black has to continue protecting the c5-bishop.} 11.O-O-O {And we will castle long now. The knight on d7 is pinned and under attack, and it is the only defender of the bishop on c5. White is slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Part 2 (5...Nbd7) / Study 2: Black's Other 6th Moves / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/451006b0-536a-461e-9ba7-797f0802c02a/studies/3899e52e-3758-47af-a6dc-22b936c84afe/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian Defense. We'll continue with the natural Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays the standard d6. We'll activate the second knight as well.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, probably not ready for what's about to come. And that's e5, of course.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nbd7 {As mentioned, 5...Nbd7 is the topic of this chapter. We'll defend the knight with d4, as seen in the 6...cxd4 study. However, in this study we will take a look at some alternatives to Black capturing in the center on move 6. It's not necessary for them to activate our queen in the center.} 6.d4 6...e6 {They can open up the dark-squared bishop quickly and look to castle. This is played 11% of the time. We'll activate the dark-squared bishop and provide more protection to the knight on e5. It helps to develop it quickly because we will castle queenside in some lines.} 7.Bf4 7...Be7 {Black continues in a solid but passive manner. They want to castle and get out of trouble. We won't make it that easy. I suggest we take on c5. Black has to recapture with one of the pieces, and it's better to recapture with the bishop. Recapturing with the knight allows a queen trade and that gives us the advantage.} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Taking with the bishop loses a tempo considering Black had just played ...Be7. Let's develop the queen and get it away from the d-file. The f3-square is probably best. We open up the d-file for our rook.} 9.Qf3 9...Nxe5 {Black will capture on e5 at some point. That's why we need the bishop on f4. We'll recapture.} 10.Bxe5 10...O-O {Black castles, and we can finish our kingside development now.} 11.Bd3 {We've suddenly got both bishops eyeing the kingside, with an active queen to help them. We are actually flexible on where we want to castle in this line, as our position is comfortable either way. There might be more pressure coming soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 5...g6 or 5...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/e449b420-4a2d-41d2-b964-a146787a6bad/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll activate our knights first.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops on f6. And now our trademark e5. We attack the knight and give Black a choice.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {They'll most likely capture. And we will take back. Now Black has a couple of options as sidelines.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nc6 {Black can offer a trade of knights on the c6-square. This is different to 5...Nbd7 because here we have the opportunity to weaken Black's pawns. This inaccuracy is played 11% of the time. We'll capture.} 6.Nxc6 6...bxc6 {The b-pawn has to take back, and look at the pawn structure now. Doubled, isolated c-pawns. We've already achieved a positional victory and we can simply develop and castle.} 7.Bc4 7...e6 {Black opens up their dark-squared bishop... But we will castle quickly. Standard stuff.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {The bishop comes out, and Black is ready to castle too. And here's a good plan for our dark-squared bishop.} 9.b3 {Instead of playing d3 and developing it that way, I suggest we fianchetto. This will take more control of the long diagonal and especially the e5-square, since we don't want Black to push ...e5. Our queen will join the game via Qf3 and target the c6-pawn. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 5...g6 or 5...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/e449b420-4a2d-41d2-b964-a146787a6bad/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll activate our knights first.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops on f6. And now our trademark e5. We attack the knight and give Black a choice.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {They'll most likely capture. And we will take back. Now Black has a couple of options as sidelines.} 5.Nxe5 5...g6 {The move ...g6 is played 13% of the time or in just over 20,000 games. It's not optimal because it gives us an easy line of attack. And that's to activate the bishop and target the f7-pawn.} 6.Bc4 6...e6 {Black pushes ...e6, blocking. But our attack is not over. The queen comes out to f3 and pressures other weak spots! In the span of just a few moves, we've developed most of the pieces and are already targeting Black's weaknesses.} 7.Qf3 7...Bg7 {Now they have time to fianchetto, because there is no immediate danger. And here I suggest an unorthodox-looking move. It's to play with the bishop again and give a check. This forces Black to block with one of their pieces and they have to be accurate in order to defend.} 8.Bb5+ 8...Nbd7 {The best move is to block with the b-knight. We'll capture, as was our idea when we played Bb5+.} 9.Bxd7+ 9...Bxd7 {Black has to recapture with the bishop. But that leaves the b7-pawn hanging. Basically, this means that Black lost a pawn by force, thanks to our fast development and the Bb5+ move.} 10.Qxb7 10...O-O {Black castles to get into safety...} 11.O-O {And so will we. White has won a pawn with no clear compensation for Black, so there is no debate about who is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 5...g6 or 5...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/e449b420-4a2d-41d2-b964-a146787a6bad/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll activate our knights first.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops on f6. And now our trademark e5. We attack the knight and give Black a choice.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {They'll most likely capture. And we will take back. Now Black has a couple of options as sidelines.} 5.Nxe5 5...g6 {The move ...g6 is played 13% of the time or in just over 20,000 games. It's not optimal because it gives us an easy line of attack. And that's to activate the bishop and target the f7-pawn.} 6.Bc4 6...e6 {Black pushes ...e6, blocking. But our attack is not over. The queen comes out to f3 and pressures other weak spots! In the span of just a few moves, we've developed most of the pieces and are already targeting Black's weaknesses.} 7.Qf3 7...Bg7 {Now they have time to fianchetto, because there is no immediate danger. And here I suggest an unorthodox-looking move. It's to play with the bishop again and give a check. This forces Black to block with one of their pieces and they have to be accurate in order to defend.} 8.Bb5+ 8...Bd7 {If they instead block with the bishop the game is over. We'll just take on b7 and attack the rook.} 9.Qxb7 9...Bxb5 {If Black captures on b5, there is something even better.} 10.Qxf7# {And that's checkmate. A win in 10 moves. Not bad.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {Black takes. And we will recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc6 {Once the knight arrives on c6, we don't have many options. We have to trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen and attack our rook. But I suggest an incredible move to remember. It's Bg5! Sacrificing the rook, we develop and attack the knight on f6. What's the idea?} 10.Bg5 10...Qxh1 {Well, if Black takes the rook... We will castle long! If you know you should play 10.Bg5, you have to understand why, and this is it. Suddenly, we are threatening to checkmate on d8 as well as to check on b5 with a discovered attack on the queen. White is winning.} 11.O-O-O 11...Bd7 {The best move is to block with ...Bd7. But that is basically giving up a piece. Because we can remove the bishop's defender with Bxf6 and threaten to capture it.} 12.Bxf6 12...Qc6 {And the beautiful thing is, if Black retreats to c6 to defend... We can sacrifice the f1-bishop! This is a common deflection tactic and transposes to the main line of this study. Black can't take because of checkmate.} 13.Bg2 13...Qd6 {They will probably slide to d6 and offer a trade. But we don't mind. Capturing on d6 is winning, too.} 14.Qxd6 14...exd6 {They recapture. And we will take on b7 now, attacking the rook. Soon the bishop will be able to give check on c6, which will prove to be very strong. Similar to the 10...Bd7 line.} 15.Bxb7 15...Rb8 {Black attacks the bishop, but we have an intermezzo. And that's the check on e1. Black has two options.} 16.Re1+ 16...Be7 {Blocking with the bishop on e7 doesn't result in checkmate immediately but it is desperate. We'll take with the rook.} 17.Rxe7+ 17...Kd8 {Black has to move to d8 not to lose the bishop on d7. But we will simply capture on f7 with check.} 18.Rxf7+ 18...gxf6 {Sure, Black can take the bishop...} 19.Bxa6 {But we will take on a6 as well and in this line we are winning because of the material advantage. Even though we are down an exchange, we are up three pawns and who is going to stop them? Besides, Black's pawns are extremely weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {Black takes. And we will recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc6 {Once the knight arrives on c6, we don't have many options. We have to trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen and attack our rook. But I suggest an incredible move to remember. It's Bg5! Sacrificing the rook, we develop and attack the knight on f6. What's the idea?} 10.Bg5 10...Qxh1 {Well, if Black takes the rook... We will castle long! If you know you should play 10.Bg5, you have to understand why, and this is it. Suddenly, we are threatening to checkmate on d8 as well as to check on b5 with a discovered attack on the queen. White is winning.} 11.O-O-O 11...Bd7 {The best move is to block with ...Bd7. But that is basically giving up a piece. Because we can remove the bishop's defender with Bxf6 and threaten to capture it.} 12.Bxf6 12...Qc6 {And the beautiful thing is, if Black retreats to c6 to defend... We can sacrifice the f1-bishop! This is a common deflection tactic and transposes to the main line of this study. Black can't take because of checkmate.} 13.Bg2 13...Qd6 {They will probably slide to d6 and offer a trade. But we don't mind. Capturing on d6 is winning, too.} 14.Qxd6 14...exd6 {They recapture. And we will take on b7 now, attacking the rook. Soon the bishop will be able to give check on c6, which will prove to be very strong. Similar to the 10...Bd7 line.} 15.Bxb7 15...Rb8 {Black attacks the bishop, but we have an intermezzo. And that's the check on e1. Black has two options.} 16.Re1+ 16...Be6 {If Black blocks with the bishop on e6, then we see why capturing on b7 came before Re1+.} 17.Bc6# {We just checkmated Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {Black takes. And we will recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc6 {Once the knight arrives on c6, we don't have many options. We have to trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen and attack our rook. But I suggest an incredible move to remember. It's Bg5! Sacrificing the rook, we develop and attack the knight on f6. What's the idea?} 10.Bg5 10...Bd7 {Black could try to place the bishop on d7 and block our queen's vision. Also, the bishop covers any potential Bb5+ ideas. But that loses the game. We will castle here. Now both the queen and the rook attack the vulnerable bishop on d7.} 11.O-O-O 11...Qxh1 {And if Black takes the rook in the corner... The attack on d7 becomes even stronger when we remove that bishop's defender. Now they can't afford to recapture our bishop.} 12.Bxf6 12...Qc6 {And the beautiful thing is, if Black retreats to c6 to defend... We can sacrifice the f1-bishop! This is a common deflection tactic and transposes to the main line of this study. Black can't take because of checkmate.} 13.Bg2 13...Qd6 {They will probably slide to d6 and offer a trade. But we don't mind. Capturing on d6 is winning, too.} 14.Qxd6 14...exd6 {They recapture. And we will take on b7 now, attacking the rook. Soon the bishop will be able to give check on c6, which will prove to be very strong. Similar to the 10...Bd7 line.} 15.Bxb7 15...Rb8 {Black attacks the bishop, but we have an intermezzo. And that's the check on e1. Black has two options.} 16.Re1+ 16...Be6 {If Black blocks with the bishop on e6, then we see why capturing on b7 came before Re1+.} 17.Bc6# {We just checkmated Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {Black takes. And we will recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc6 {Once the knight arrives on c6, we don't have many options. We have to trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen and attack our rook. But I suggest an incredible move to remember. It's Bg5! Sacrificing the rook, we develop and attack the knight on f6. What's the idea?} 10.Bg5 10...Qxh1 {Well, if Black takes the rook... We will castle long! If you know you should play 10.Bg5, you have to understand why, and this is it. Suddenly, we are threatening to checkmate on d8 as well as to check on b5 with a discovered attack on the queen. White is winning.} 11.O-O-O 11...Nd7 {They can try to hold and defend against both threats with ...Nd7.} 12.Nd5 {But we will just make another threat with Nd5. This is way too much for Black to deal with.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {Black takes. And we will recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc6 {Once the knight arrives on c6, we don't have many options. We have to trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen and attack our rook. But I suggest an incredible move to remember. It's Bg5! Sacrificing the rook, we develop and attack the knight on f6. What's the idea?} 10.Bg5 10...Qxh1 {Well, if Black takes the rook... We will castle long! If you know you should play 10.Bg5, you have to understand why, and this is it. Suddenly, we are threatening to checkmate on d8 as well as to check on b5 with a discovered attack on the queen. White is winning.} 11.O-O-O 11...Bd7 {The best move is to block with ...Bd7. But that is basically giving up a piece. Because we can remove the bishop's defender with Bxf6 and threaten to capture it.} 12.Bxf6 12...Qc6 {And the beautiful thing is, if Black retreats to c6 to defend... We can sacrifice the f1-bishop! This is a common deflection tactic and transposes to the main line of this study. Black can't take because of checkmate.} 13.Bg2 13...Qc8 {If they move to c8, we will explain how White is winning in a bit more depth than the 10...Bd7 line. We'll take on g7 and attack the rook. It doesn't make much sense for Black to capture on g7 as our queen would invade and it would be even tougher to defend.} 14.Bxg7 14...Rg8 {So they will probably slide to g8. After which we can take the bishop.} 15.Bxf8 15...Rxf8 {The idea is that once the rook recaptures...} 16.Nd5 {We've got this Nd5 move and we are getting in. The knight is threatening to jump to b6, and there are ideas of placing a piece on the e-file and threatening Nf6 if Black defends with something like ...Qd8. We're completely winning even though down an exchange.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {Black takes. And we will recapture.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc6 {Once the knight arrives on c6, we don't have many options. We have to trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen and attack our rook. But I suggest an incredible move to remember. It's Bg5! Sacrificing the rook, we develop and attack the knight on f6. What's the idea?} 10.Bg5 10...Bg4 {The best move for Black is actually ...Bg4. The bishop is preventing us from castling and Black awaits our response.} 11.Bxf6 {We'll take on f6. Black's best response here is the unfathomable 11...h5!! (preventing Qxg4) and even after this we'll keep up the pressure with 12.Nd5. Black will have a hard time defending.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Nbd7 {A move that's played 24% of the time is ...Nbd7. Black offers a trade of knights this way, but we have already seen a similar position. Our go-to move is d4 to expand and protect the knight.} 7.d4 7...Nxe5 {Sure, Black can trade here. But they won't get much out of it. We'll take and hit the f6-knight.} 8.dxe5 8...Qxd1+ {Black can even trade the queens. After this, we will recapture with the knight and preserve castling rights. We also want to keep protecting the f2-square.} 9.Nxd1 9...Ng4 {With f2 protected, the knight moving to g4 doesn't achieve anything.} 10.f4 {The f4-push protects the e5-pawn and we create a nice and stable pawn chain. Behind it, we will activate the light-squared bishop. White is slightly better already.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Nbd7 {A move that's played 24% of the time is ...Nbd7. Black offers a trade of knights this way, but we have already seen a similar position. Our go-to move is d4 to expand and protect the knight.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {The most natural move is to take the pawn here. But we should already be familar with a position like this. We'll take with the queen, protecting the knight.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nxe5 {The knight captures on e5. We'll simply take back.} 9.Qxe5 9...e6 {And once a move like ...e6 arrives...} 10.Bf4 {We can get the bishop to f4 and prevent Black from activating to the most optimal square. Next, we will simply fianchetto and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 1) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/394796be-0ae4-4df8-a932-22d0362dcfc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian is on the board. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the king's knight. We'll play our trademark e5 push.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. We'll recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {And now Black plays 5...a6. This move is only played 8% of the time, but serves a specific purpose. And that's to take away the b5-square from our minor pieces. We've seen the danger this can pose in previous studies. We'll take this opportunity to play g3 and fianchetto our bishop. It will be as strong on g2 as it would have been elsewhere.} 6.g3 6...Qc7 {A move that is played 19% of the time is ...Qc7, attacking the knight. Black's queen is safe and posing problems. We don't want to move the knight. We will push d4 instead. Our idea is not only to protect the knight, but to play Bf4 and target the queen.} 7.d4 7...e6 {Black could play ...e6 here. The idea is to meet our potential Bf4 with ...Bd6. We will retreat to c4 instead. Now our knight controls the d6-square and we want to play Bf4, causing disruption to the black position.} 8.Nc4 8...cxd4 {If Black takes in the center... We'll simply recapture with the queen. It could become a target for a short amount of time...} 9.Qxd4 9...Bc5 {If Black plays ...Bc5. But we will simply offer a trade on f4. If Black takes, our bishop will get developed actively.} 10.Qf4 10...Qc6 {And if they don't, our queen is still active on f4. Our rook on h1 is now under attack.} 11.Rg1 {We'll simply slide it to g1. We've got a lot of ideas incoming, like Bg2 to hit the queen again, as well as Be3 to trade the dark-squared bishops. Black's bishop on c8 is still passive. We'll castle queenside, with an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 3: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 2) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/a7d5389b-478b-4edf-9c5b-ff815c1e79b2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays ...c5 and takes control of d4. We'll take some control back with Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns with ...d6. And we will develop the queenside knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Once the knight arrives at f6... We'll push e5 and attack it.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {Black goes for ...a6, preventing our ideas where a minor piece comes to b5. We will develop with g3 and prepare to fianchetto the bishop. Let's go over some of Black's options in this study.} 6.g3 6...Qd6 {Objectively the best move for Black here is actually ...Qd6. The idea is that it attacks the knight and prevents d4. Also, there are ideas of ...Nc6 and Black taking over the diagonal. This move is very rare and played only 3% of the time. We will get our own queen out and protect the knight: we don't mind blocking the bishop as it is going to activate on g2.} 7.Qe2 7...Nc6 {Again, the best approach is ...Nc6 here. Not only does Black practically force a trade of knights, they are also threatening to jump to the d4-square. We'll take.} 8.Nxc6 8...Qxc6 {The queen recaptures and attacks our rook, which blocks our fianchetto idea for now. We have to react.} 9.Rg1 {Instead of blocking with f3, simply sliding the rook to g1 is fine here. We'll fianchetto and take back the diagonal. The position is equal.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 3: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 2) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/a7d5389b-478b-4edf-9c5b-ff815c1e79b2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays ...c5 and takes control of d4. We'll take some control back with Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns with ...d6. And we will develop the queenside knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Once the knight arrives at f6... We'll push e5 and attack it.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {Black goes for ...a6, preventing our ideas where a minor piece comes to b5. We will develop with g3 and prepare to fianchetto the bishop. Let's go over some of Black's options in this study.} 6.g3 6...Qd6 {Objectively the best move for Black here is actually ...Qd6. The idea is that it attacks the knight and prevents d4. Also, there are ideas of ...Nc6 and Black taking over the diagonal. This move is very rare and played only 3% of the time. We will get our own queen out and protect the knight: we don't mind blocking the bishop as it is going to activate on g2.} 7.Qe2 7...e6 {Black can push ...e6 here as well. Nothing confrontational here and we can simply continue with our plan.} 8.Bg2 {And that's to fianchetto the bishop. We've got a lot of ideas here, including a4 to expand on the queenside, since Black can't play ...b5. In the center, d3 and Bf4 would be a great idea to play against the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 3: Black Plays 5...a6 (Part 2) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/a7d5389b-478b-4edf-9c5b-ff815c1e79b2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays ...c5 and takes control of d4. We'll take some control back with Nf3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns with ...d6. And we will develop the queenside knight.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Once the knight arrives at f6... We'll push e5 and attack it.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back.} 5.Nxe5 5...a6 {Black goes for ...a6, preventing our ideas where a minor piece comes to b5. We will develop with g3 and prepare to fianchetto the bishop. Let's go over some of Black's options in this study.} 6.g3 6...g6 {Black can choose to mirror our approach here, although that is played only 6% of the time. It is a solid setup in which both sides will simply develop without much confrontation. Let's activate the bishop first.} 7.Bg2 7...Bg7 {Black does the same, and both sides are ready to castle. We'll get our king into safety.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Black castles as well. Now that the kingside is safe and there isn't much fight in the center, how should we approach the position? A good way to try and expand on the queenside is a4. We are guaranteed some space there.} 9.a4 9...Qc7 {Sure, Black can go after our knight, but we have a natural defensive move.} 10.Re1 {And that's to place the rook on the e1-square. We'll try to harass that queen with d3 and Bf4 next to grab some tempi. On the queenside, a5 is a good idea. Then, we can bring the knight to c4 and potentially to b6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qc7 {A move that's played 7% of the time and seems to make sense is ...Qc7. Black attacks our knight from a safe square. However, unlike 5...Qd6, the queen doesn't cover d4. Which means we will play it. We defend the knight and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {The most natural move is for Black to take this pawn. We'll recapture with the queen and keep the knight defended.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nc6 {Black can try to attack both, but we have only one option here. And a good one. That's to trade the knights.} 8.Nxc6 8...bxc6 {Black has to take with the pawn, which gives us freedom to develop with tempo now that the knights have been traded. And we'll attack the queen with Bf4.} 9.Bf4 9...Qb6 {This line doesn't win on the spot for us, but we will be better. Black can offer a trade of queens here.} 10.O-O-O {And we'll let them trade if they want to. Black's weak pawns and our lead in activity and development make our position better even without the queens on the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qd6 {Black chooses to attack our knight with the queen and forces it to move, since the queen also prevents d4. It's played 2% of the time. We'll jump to c4 and attack the queen right away. The best move for Black here might even be to retreat all the way to d8.} 6.Nc4 6...Qe6+ {But I doubt many players will do that. Although this check seems logical, it's an inaccuracy. Black blocks the e-pawn. Be2 is a simple way for us to block the check.} 7.Be2 7...Nc6 {Sure, they can continue developing now, thinking they've grabbed some momentum. But the queen leaving the d6-square like this gives us another idea. And that's Nb5. Why shouldn't we play with the knight and threaten a fork? Black is the one that abandoned the c7-square after all.} 8.Nb5 8...Qd7 {Now the queen retreating to d7 is the best way to defend. But Black blocks the light-squared bishop and has already move the queen three times. Let's gambit a pawn on d4! This works because we want to eventually place the bishop on f4 and threaten the fork on c7 again. Besides, if Black accepts, they will spend another move on that and we will easily and rapidly develop.} 9.d4 9...cxd4 {If Black takes with the pawn, I suggest we go for the aforementioned idea. And that's to place the bishop on f4, reintroducing the threat of the c7-fork again.} 10.Bf4 10...Nd5 {Black can stop it for now...} 11.Bg3 {But our position is still better. Black's pieces are unnaturally placed and tangled up, and they will need to take a few more moves to castle. Meanwhile, we will castle right away and maintain a lead in activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qd6 {Black chooses to attack our knight with the queen and forces it to move, since the queen also prevents d4. It's played 2% of the time. We'll jump to c4 and attack the queen right away. The best move for Black here might even be to retreat all the way to d8.} 6.Nc4 6...Qe6+ {But I doubt many players will do that. Although this check seems logical, it's an inaccuracy. Black blocks the e-pawn. Be2 is a simple way for us to block the check.} 7.Be2 7...Nc6 {Sure, they can continue developing now, thinking they've grabbed some momentum. But the queen leaving the d6-square like this gives us another idea. And that's Nb5. Why shouldn't we play with the knight and threaten a fork? Black is the one that abandoned the c7-square after all.} 8.Nb5 8...Qd7 {Now the queen retreating to d7 is the best way to defend. But Black blocks the light-squared bishop and has already move the queen three times. Let's gambit a pawn on d4! This works because we want to eventually place the bishop on f4 and threaten the fork on c7 again. Besides, if Black accepts, they will spend another move on that and we will easily and rapidly develop.} 9.d4 9...Nxd4 {Black could take with the knight, hoping for a trade on d4. But we will not let them off that easily. First of all, let's bring the knight back to e5 and attack the queen again. Now that we also have a knight on b5, Black can't return to d6 and still has to keep an eye on the c7-square.} 10.Ne5 10...Qd8 {So the only good square is d8. But now we don't have to play Bf4.} 11.Be3 {Placing the bishop on e3 is actually sufficient. We attack the knight and threaten to get our pawn back. Note that Black can't capture the bishop on e2 because we could trade the queens and then fork with Nxf7+.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qc7 {A move that's played 7% of the time and seems to make sense is ...Qc7. Black attacks our knight from a safe square. However, unlike 5...Qd6, the queen doesn't cover d4. Which means we will play it. We defend the knight and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {The most natural move is for Black to take this pawn. We'll recapture with the queen and keep the knight defended.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nc6 {Black can try to attack both, but we have only one option here. And a good one. That's to trade the knights.} 8.Nxc6 8...Qxc6 {Recapturing with the queen loses on the spot. Do you see the tactic?} 9.Bb5 {Pin and win. A free queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Bf5 {A move that immediately and directly gives us the advantage is ...Bf5. It seems like a natural developing move but we have an antidote for it. This move is played only 3% of the time. The move we will play is Qf3. It simply forks the bishop and the pawn on b7 and Black has to react to it.} 6.Qf3 6...Qc8 {The best defense is ...Qc8, covering both threats.} 7.Bc4 {But we'll continue with active attacking moves. In this instance, we'll threaten to capture on f7. Apart from that, a good idea to expand would be the simple d3 in the center, and the aggressive g4 on the kingside. We want to destabilize Black's defenders.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qc7 {A move that's played 7% of the time and seems to make sense is ...Qc7. Black attacks our knight from a safe square. However, unlike 5...Qd6, the queen doesn't cover d4. Which means we will play it. We defend the knight and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 6.d4 6...e6 {6...e6 in this position is simply passive and gives us a lot of room to attack. And we will do that with Bf4. Now the threat is Ng6, attacking both the queen and the rook on h8.} 7.Bf4 7...Bd6 {And Black can't even block this with ...Bd6. This is a huge blunder.} 8.Nb5 {Because we have a fork on b5 and Black is lost. If they retreat to d8, for example, we can even sacrifice on f7 before capturing on d6 with check. There are many winning moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Various Move 5 Sidelines / Study 4: Black Plays 5…Qc7, 5...Bf5, or 5...Qd6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/fc533064-7889-40fd-9322-3b7d51c50efe/studies/4c665ab9-7fbd-419e-939a-4874f30ffb79/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll play the most usual response.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black plays in a standard way too. We'll get both knights out.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {And now Black activates their knight to f6. Let's go for our standard move in this course, which is e5.} 4.e5 4...dxe5 {Black captures. And we'll take back. Let's examine some further sidelines from Black here.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qc7 {A move that's played 7% of the time and seems to make sense is ...Qc7. Black attacks our knight from a safe square. However, unlike 5...Qd6, the queen doesn't cover d4. Which means we will play it. We defend the knight and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {The most natural move is for Black to take this pawn. We'll recapture with the queen and keep the knight defended.} 7.Qxd4 7...e6 {Again, ...e6 doesn't work in this position either.} 8.Nb5 {We have the dangerous Nb5 and we are breaking through. The knight is threatening the queen as well as to jump to the d6-square. Black doesn't have many options for the queen and is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...a6 {If our bishop is attacked, we can simply capture. 4...a6 is played just under 15% of the time. This will also disrupt Black's pawn structure.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {They take and now the pawns are doubled on the c-file. However, they might be aiming for ...d5 next. That's why I suggest pushing e5 here. This threatens to split Black's pawns even further and also restricts potential development with ...Nf6.} 6.e5 6...d5 {A more reasonable move than capturing on e5 is Black pushing ...d5. We allowed them to grab space but they still have the doubled pawns and it's going to be harder for them to develop. Let's take away the g4-square from Black's bishop. This is useful because they can't develop it with ease now.} 7.h3 7...e6 {Which might provoke ...e6. Now that bishop is in jail. We can just castle here since we finished kingside development. Next, we should get our dark-squared bishop out.} 8.O-O 8...Ne7 {Black develops their knight.} 9.b3 {We'll bring the bishop to b2 or a3. This move also covers the c4-square. We can get the bishop to a3 with the knight on a4 to target the c5-pawn, for example. Simple development on b2 is also fine. White has a nice advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Nf6 {Now if Black goes for ...Nf6 that takes us back to familiar territory. This move is played just over 10% of the time. Let's push and attack the knight.} 5.e5 5...dxe5 {Black captures. Of course, we'll take back. Now there is a lot of pressure on the pinned knight on c6.} 6.Nxe5 6...Bd7 {Black blocks with ...Bd7. But we can trade everything. Let's take with the bishop first.} 7.Bxc6 7...Bxc6 {Black recaptures. And we'll trade again.} 8.Nxc6 8...bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures...} 9.Qf3 {And now our queen gets out to f3. The whole point of us trading these pieces is that the a- and the c-pawns are now isolated and extremely weak. They are going to be our primary targets and the source of advantage. White is already better.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...a6 {If our bishop is attacked, we can simply capture. 4...a6 is played just under 15% of the time. This will also disrupt Black's pawn structure.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {They take and now the pawns are doubled on the c-file. However, they might be aiming for ...d5 next. That's why I suggest pushing e5 here. This threatens to split Black's pawns even further and also restricts potential development with ...Nf6.} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Capturing on e5 is simply bad for Black.} 7.Nxe5 {We'll just take back and play against their weak pawns. Our queen can develop to f3 and we can strengthen our position with d3. Our bishop can also come out via b3 and Bb2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {A move that definitely goes according to Black's plan is ...Bd7, unpinning. It's played more than half the time. They want to keep an eye on that e5-square. But we will trade on c6. In this line, we play Bb5 with the intention of removing the knight.} 5.Bxc6 5...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the bishop provides more resistance. We don't want to push e5 now and end up losing the g2-pawn. Let's expand in the center instead. This is another benefit of trading on c6.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black takes, Sicilian style. And we'll recapture with the knight. Now the bishop is under attack, but it's not our goal to capture it right away.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nf6 {If Black develops, we don't have to rush with capturing the bishop. Instead, the position could transform into a developing battle. Let's defend the e4-pawn first.} 8.Qe2 8...g6 {Black could choose to go for the fianchetto here. That is definitely a sound plan. In which case we will solidify our position with f3. We want to play this before Be3 to deter any potential ...Ng4. Also, this strengthens the e4-pawn.} 9.f3 9...Bg7 {Black activates the bishop. So will we.} 10.Be3 10...O-O {Castling kingside.} 11.O-O-O {But we will castle queenside. Similar to the 7...Bd7 line, our plan is to push on the kingside and attack, while Black will try to do the same on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {A move that definitely goes according to Black's plan is ...Bd7, unpinning. It's played more than half the time. They want to keep an eye on that e5-square. But we will trade on c6. In this line, we play Bb5 with the intention of removing the knight.} 5.Bxc6 5...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the bishop provides more resistance. We don't want to push e5 now and end up losing the g2-pawn. Let's expand in the center instead. This is another benefit of trading on c6.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black takes, Sicilian style. And we'll recapture with the knight. Now the bishop is under attack, but it's not our goal to capture it right away.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nf6 {If Black develops, we don't have to rush with capturing the bishop. Instead, the position could transform into a developing battle. Let's defend the e4-pawn first.} 8.Qe2 8...e5 {Advancing in the center only encourages us to capture on c6. And we will.} 9.Nxc6 9...bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures, and we don't really have to worry about ...d5. It would destabilize the e5-pawn too much. We can simply develop. The bishop is coming to b2 and we will castle long.} 10.b3 10...Be7 {Black prepares to castle as well. And we'll fianchetto. Now the bishop is targeting the center.} 11.Bb2 11...O-O {Why did we choose to castle long when Black castles short?} 12.O-O-O {Well, by now you must realize that it's a common motif in this study. When there is opposide side castling on the board, it's much easier to create a pawn storm. Here, we want to play g3 and f4 to open up the g-file for our rook and open up our bishop. Our d-rook can move to d3 either to slide to the kingside or double up with the other rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {A move that definitely goes according to Black's plan is ...Bd7, unpinning. It's played more than half the time. They want to keep an eye on that e5-square. But we will trade on c6. In this line, we play Bb5 with the intention of removing the knight.} 5.Bxc6 5...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the bishop provides more resistance. We don't want to push e5 now and end up losing the g2-pawn. Let's expand in the center instead. This is another benefit of trading on c6.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black takes, Sicilian style. And we'll recapture with the knight. Now the bishop is under attack, but it's not our goal to capture it right away.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nf6 {If Black develops, we don't have to rush with capturing the bishop. Instead, the position could transform into a developing battle. Let's defend the e4-pawn first.} 8.Qe2 8...e6 {Going for ...e6 is not the optimal approach for Black. If they want to activate the dark-squared bishop, it's easier to do so with ...g6 or the aggressive ...e5 than try to untangle in the center like this. We can develop and target the weakened d6-pawn.} 9.Bf4 9...e5 {This might provoke Black to push ...e5, thinking they've got a fork. But we'll just take on c6.} 10.Nxc6 10...bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures. And now we can castle and keep an eye on the d-file where Black has the backwards d-pawn. But wait! We've hung the bishop on f4!} 11.O-O-O 11...Qc7 {It's better for Black not to capture on f4 and get the queen off the d-file. In which case we can slide the bishop to g5 and hit the knight.} 12.Bg5 12...Be7 {The bishop defends.} 13.Kb1 {But the d6-pawn is still weak and we can slide to b1 prophylactically. Next, we will look to play against that weakness as much as we can. We're slightly better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {A move that definitely goes according to Black's plan is ...Bd7, unpinning. It's played more than half the time. They want to keep an eye on that e5-square. But we will trade on c6. In this line, we play Bb5 with the intention of removing the knight.} 5.Bxc6 5...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the bishop provides more resistance. We don't want to push e5 now and end up losing the g2-pawn. Let's expand in the center instead. This is another benefit of trading on c6.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black takes, Sicilian style. And we'll recapture with the knight. Now the bishop is under attack, but it's not our goal to capture it right away.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nf6 {If Black develops, we don't have to rush with capturing the bishop. Instead, the position could transform into a developing battle. Let's defend the e4-pawn first.} 8.Qe2 8...e6 {Going for ...e6 is not the optimal approach for Black. If they want to activate the dark-squared bishop, it's easier to do so with ...g6 or the aggressive ...e5 than try to untangle in the center like this. We can develop and target the weakened d6-pawn.} 9.Bf4 9...e5 {This might provoke Black to push ...e5, thinking they've got a fork. But we'll just take on c6.} 10.Nxc6 10...bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures. And now we can castle and keep an eye on the d-file where Black has the backwards d-pawn. But wait! We've hung the bishop on f4!} 11.O-O-O 11...exf4 {If Black takes, we have immediate play. We'll push e5 and attack the knight. The d-pawn is pinned to the queen.} 12.e5 12...Qe7 {Black will most likely play ...Qe7 to prevent us from capturing on d6 with discovered check. But we have a nice move to keep the advantage.} 13.Qa6 {And that's Qa6. The knight on f6 is still under attack, and now we are threatening to capture on c6 with a fork as well. Meanwhile, our rook is coming to e1 to dominate the e-file. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {A move that definitely goes according to Black's plan is ...Bd7, unpinning. It's played more than half the time. They want to keep an eye on that e5-square. But we will trade on c6. In this line, we play Bb5 with the intention of removing the knight.} 5.Bxc6 5...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the bishop provides more resistance. We don't want to push e5 now and end up losing the g2-pawn. Let's expand in the center instead. This is another benefit of trading on c6.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black takes, Sicilian style. And we'll recapture with the knight. Now the bishop is under attack, but it's not our goal to capture it right away.} 7.Nxd4 7...Bd7 {Black retreating is extremely passive and gives us a tempo. We can just develop the bishop. This line is probably the most Sicilian-looking line in the course. It's going to be very similar to the Dragon Sicilian.} 8.Be3 8...Nf6 {Black activates the knight to f3. Since our bishop is on e3, they might want to jump to g4 and target it. We don't want that since we will need our bishop. So f3 is in order.} 9.f3 9...g6 {Black's idea is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and castle. Since the c-file is open, they want to have a strong bishop on g7 and a rook on c8 to target our queenside, which is probably where we will castle. Meanwhile, we will prepare to castle queenside while creating a battery on the dark squares with Qd2. It's designed to trade off Black's dark-squared bishop.} 10.Qd2 10...Bg7 {They decide to fianchetto now. And both sides will castle.} 11.O-O-O 11...O-O {But on opposite sides of the board.} 12.Bh6 {This usually signifies a sharp battle, and that is what the Dragon comes down to. We will trade off the bishops and look to quickly push g4 and h4 to attack on the kingside. Meanwhile, Black will gravitate towards the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 1: Black Plays The Popular 3...Nc6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/d7e53e60-fb9d-4131-b291-ff33899fe136/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black connects their pawns. We'll get both knights out. And Black doesn't have to play ...Nf6 here.} 3.Nc3 3...Nc6 {They can also develop the queenside knight, and they do so 27% of the time, in more than 3.3 million games. This could be designed to prevent our e5-expansion if ...Nf6 comes. Also, the knight covers d4. However, we can pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {A move that definitely goes according to Black's plan is ...Bd7, unpinning. It's played more than half the time. They want to keep an eye on that e5-square. But we will trade on c6. In this line, we play Bb5 with the intention of removing the knight.} 5.Bxc6 5...bxc6 {Recapturing with the pawn is illogical. Black worsens their structure for no reason. And we can play e5 with ease.} 6.e5 6...d5 {If Black pushes ...d5, the position is similar to the 4...a6 line.} 7.b3 {We'll play b3 and look to target that c5-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 2: Black Avoids Our Course (3...e6, 3...a6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/e27a715f-f8b0-4ac6-b8c9-94a54414ad4b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black goes for ...d6. Our two knights are now out. Let's look at some other third move deviations.} 3.Nc3 3...a6 {Black could play ...a6 already on move 3 to take away the b5-square from our pieces. But that wastes a precious tempo and gives us time to expand in the center. This move is only played 8% of the time. Let's go for d4.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {After Black captures, it might seem like we want a standard Sicilian by recapturing with the knight. But we will actually take with the queen! This will definitely spice things up. If Black allows it to remain active, we've already seen what the queen can do in previous chapters.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {Instead, expect Black to attack the queen right away. We won't retreat all the way to d1 but rather to d3. This helps our queen stay somewhat active.} 6.Qd3 6...Nf6 {Black continues developing. And we'll offer a knight trade here. This might help us grab space and improve the mobility of our pieces.} 7.Nd5 7...e6 {Black doesn't have to trade and let us get what we want. Instead, they can force our hand. But we are ready for that too. Let's take the knight on f6. The idea is that we induced Black to weaken their structure by playing both ...d6 and ...e6 and now the d6-pawn will be weak.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures, and now we would love to activate the dark-squared bishop, but the b2-pawn would be hanging. So c3 makes sense. We are now aiming to eventually play against the d6-weakness.} 9.c3 9...h6 {Black prevents Bg5 with this logical move. So we will just continue developing. The e2-square is fine for the bishop as it covers g4 from there. And you will see our plan soon.} 10.Be2 10...Be7 {Black prepares to castle... And so will we.} 11.Be3 11...O-O {But after Black castles, I suggest an immediate pawn advance...} 12.g4 {In the form of g4. Since the queen is on f6, it's an excellent target for g5. If the g-file opens, we will be in good shape. On the other hand, we will castle queenside to bring the king to safety.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 2: Black Avoids Our Course (3...e6, 3...a6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/e27a715f-f8b0-4ac6-b8c9-94a54414ad4b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black goes for ...d6. Our two knights are now out. Let's look at some other third move deviations.} 3.Nc3 3...a6 {Black could play ...a6 already on move 3 to take away the b5-square from our pieces. But that wastes a precious tempo and gives us time to expand in the center. This move is only played 8% of the time. Let's go for d4.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {After Black captures, it might seem like we want a standard Sicilian by recapturing with the knight. But we will actually take with the queen! This will definitely spice things up. If Black allows it to remain active, we've already seen what the queen can do in previous chapters.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {Instead, expect Black to attack the queen right away. We won't retreat all the way to d1 but rather to d3. This helps our queen stay somewhat active.} 6.Qd3 6...Nf6 {Black continues developing. And we'll offer a knight trade here. This might help us grab space and improve the mobility of our pieces.} 7.Nd5 7...e6 {Black doesn't have to trade and let us get what we want. Instead, they can force our hand. But we are ready for that too. Let's take the knight on f6. The idea is that we induced Black to weaken their structure by playing both ...d6 and ...e6 and now the d6-pawn will be weak.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures, and now we would love to activate the dark-squared bishop, but the b2-pawn would be hanging. So c3 makes sense. We are now aiming to eventually play against the d6-weakness.} 9.c3 9...Be7 {We don't need to rush with Bg5 if Black goes to e7. Simple development will suffice. Let's activate the light-squared bishop first.} 10.Be2 10...O-O {Once Black castles...} 11.Be3 {We will do the same with the dark-squared bishop. We could choose to castle queenside and have a sharp battle trying to push the pawns on opposite sides. But we could also castle short and play more of a positional game. White is slightly better, but nothing substantial yet.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 2: Black Avoids Our Course (3...e6, 3...a6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/e27a715f-f8b0-4ac6-b8c9-94a54414ad4b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black goes for ...d6. Our two knights are now out. Let's look at some other third move deviations.} 3.Nc3 3...a6 {Black could play ...a6 already on move 3 to take away the b5-square from our pieces. But that wastes a precious tempo and gives us time to expand in the center. This move is only played 8% of the time. Let's go for d4.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {After Black captures, it might seem like we want a standard Sicilian by recapturing with the knight. But we will actually take with the queen! This will definitely spice things up. If Black allows it to remain active, we've already seen what the queen can do in previous chapters.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {Instead, expect Black to attack the queen right away. We won't retreat all the way to d1 but rather to d3. This helps our queen stay somewhat active.} 6.Qd3 6...Nf6 {Black continues developing. And we'll offer a knight trade here. This might help us grab space and improve the mobility of our pieces.} 7.Nd5 7...Nxd5 {Black capturing works out great for us. We'll take back with the pawn and hit the knight.} 8.exd5 8...Nb4 {If it jumps to b4, we can simply slide to the queenside as mentioned. Nb4 is not a good move. From b3, our queen attacks the knight and takes aim at b7.} 9.Qb3 9...a5 {Black can maintain the knight, but only for one move. We will kick it out immediately with a3.} 10.a3 10...Na6 {Black retreats the knight to a6. They might want to bring it to c5. So let's develop accordingly.} 11.Be3 {We'll activate the bishop to e3 and cover the c5-square. Our pieces are active and we'll probably castle queenside. White is winning!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 2: Black Avoids Our Course (3...e6, 3...a6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/e27a715f-f8b0-4ac6-b8c9-94a54414ad4b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black goes for ...d6. Our two knights are now out. Let's look at some other third move deviations.} 3.Nc3 3...a6 {Black could play ...a6 already on move 3 to take away the b5-square from our pieces. But that wastes a precious tempo and gives us time to expand in the center. This move is only played 8% of the time. Let's go for d4.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {After Black captures, it might seem like we want a standard Sicilian by recapturing with the knight. But we will actually take with the queen! This will definitely spice things up. If Black allows it to remain active, we've already seen what the queen can do in previous chapters.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {Instead, expect Black to attack the queen right away. We won't retreat all the way to d1 but rather to d3. This helps our queen stay somewhat active.} 6.Qd3 6...Nf6 {Black continues developing. And we'll offer a knight trade here. This might help us grab space and improve the mobility of our pieces.} 7.Nd5 7...Nxd5 {Black capturing works out great for us. We'll take back with the pawn and hit the knight.} 8.exd5 8...Ne5 {Black jumps into the center. It's definitely advantageous for us to trade here.} 9.Nxe5 9...dxe5 {Now Black's pawns are doubled.} 10.Qb3 {We can go ahead with the Qb3 idea. The queen eyes the b7-pawn in case Black develops the c8-bishop. We'll enhance that threat with the Be2-Bf3 maneuver.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 2: Black Avoids Our Course (3...e6, 3...a6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/e27a715f-f8b0-4ac6-b8c9-94a54414ad4b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black goes for ...d6. Our two knights are now out. Let's look at some other third move deviations.} 3.Nc3 3...e6 {The move ...e6 might be designed to try and prepare ...d5, although that looks risky. Otherwise, maybe Black just wants to stay solid and play a small center game. This is played in 10% of games. In some cases, the d6-pawn could be a target when they play ...e6 like this, however. Either way, it gives us space and time to push d4.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes. And we'll recapture with the queen! Since Black hasn't played ...a6, taking with the queen here might prove to be powerful.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {Since we can pin the Black knight once it arrives on c6. We will play Bb5. This way our queen can stay in the center for longer and allow for active central play.} 6.Bb5 6...a6 {Attacking the bishop now doesn't make much of a difference in our approach. We have to capture.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures and now Black has three pawns on the sixth rank which do restrict our knights. However, they also restrict Black's own bishops. All in all, we have three developed pieces so far against Black's none. And with b3, we'll aim to develop a fourth one.} 8.b3 8...Nf6 {Black can try to place the knight on f6, but as soon as they do that, remember...} 9.e5 {To push e5! With the similar idea as in any other e5-line in this course, we want to attack the knight and potentially split Black's pawns. White is slightly better, but Black can probably equalize with perfect play.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 2: Black Avoids Our Course (3...e6, 3...a6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/e27a715f-f8b0-4ac6-b8c9-94a54414ad4b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black plays the Sicilian. We'll develop the king's knight.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {Black goes for ...d6. Our two knights are now out. Let's look at some other third move deviations.} 3.Nc3 3...e6 {The move ...e6 might be designed to try and prepare ...d5, although that looks risky. Otherwise, maybe Black just wants to stay solid and play a small center game. This is played in 10% of games. In some cases, the d6-pawn could be a target when they play ...e6 like this, however. Either way, it gives us space and time to push d4.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes. And we'll recapture with the queen! Since Black hasn't played ...a6, taking with the queen here might prove to be powerful.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {Since we can pin the Black knight once it arrives on c6. We will play Bb5. This way our queen can stay in the center for longer and allow for active central play.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {If Black chooses to unpin here, we don't have to rush and capture the knight right away. Simply sliding to d3 is good because we keep an attack on the d6-pawn and look to place the bishop on f4 to add an attacker.} 7.Qd3 7...Nf6 {Black can develop all they want, but we have our plan. After Bf4, the pawn is attacked twice. And if needed, we can bring the rook to d1 to attack it the third time.} 8.Bf4 8...e5 {Which is why Black will probably have to push ...e5 to save the pawn. But that has its disadvantages, of course.} 9.Bg5 {We can simply slide to g5 and pin the knight. Now there is a big hole on the d5-square which we will definitely use. If Black doesn't react, we can even place the knight on d5 on the next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 3: Black Plays The 3...g6 System / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/5d423945-0faf-4f28-8d37-65603ff02571/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black connects their pawns. Let's get the other knight to c3 now. We are now going to take a look at a specific variation Black can play.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {It's 3...g6. Black can choose to fianchetto the bishop in basically any Sicilian variation, so this is something we have to cover here as well. Our main antidote to this will be to immediately push d4 and try to get the queen to the center to counteract the bishop. 3...g6 is played just over 5% of the time, in more than 785,000 games.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures. And we will take back with the queen, as mentioned. Black still can't fianchetto.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nf6 {The most natural response is for Black to develop the knight, blocking our queen's vision, and prepare to fianchetto on the next move. This is by far the most common move, played 72% of the time. Let's continue in Anti-Sicilian style, and push e5 now that we see the knight on f6.} 6.e5 6...Nc6 {Black can for an intermezzo here, forking our queen and the pawn on e5. But we will simply pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 7.Bb5 7...dxe5 {If Black captures on e5 now, our plans don't change much compared to the 6...dxe5 line. We will trade the queens first.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Kxd8 {Black has to recapture with the king. Now the knight is not pinned anymore and is actually protecting the e5-pawn. So we will take it as well.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {The pawn recaptures. And now there is an even bigger threat after we capture on e5. We are threatening both a fork and the pawn on c6.} 10.Nxe5 10...Ke8 {Don't rush with capturing on c6 if Black slides to e8. If we take now, there is ...Bb7 with a skewer.} 11.Bd2 {We can just develop and prepare to castle on the next move. c6 remains a permanent weakness for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 3: Black Plays The 3...g6 System / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/5d423945-0faf-4f28-8d37-65603ff02571/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black connects their pawns. Let's get the other knight to c3 now. We are now going to take a look at a specific variation Black can play.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {It's 3...g6. Black can choose to fianchetto the bishop in basically any Sicilian variation, so this is something we have to cover here as well. Our main antidote to this will be to immediately push d4 and try to get the queen to the center to counteract the bishop. 3...g6 is played just over 5% of the time, in more than 785,000 games.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures. And we will take back with the queen, as mentioned. Black still can't fianchetto.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nf6 {The most natural response is for Black to develop the knight, blocking our queen's vision, and prepare to fianchetto on the next move. This is by far the most common move, played 72% of the time. Let's continue in Anti-Sicilian style, and push e5 now that we see the knight on f6.} 6.e5 6...Nc6 {Black can for an intermezzo here, forking our queen and the pawn on e5. But we will simply pin the knight with Bb5 and neutralize it.} 7.Bb5 7...dxe5 {If Black captures on e5 now, our plans don't change much compared to the 6...dxe5 line. We will trade the queens first.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Kxd8 {Black has to recapture with the king. Now the knight is not pinned anymore and is actually protecting the e5-pawn. So we will take it as well.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {The pawn recaptures. And now there is an even bigger threat after we capture on e5. We are threatening both a fork and the pawn on c6.} 10.Nxe5 10...Be6 {If Black commits the bishop to e6... We can actually capture on c6 with check and there is no ...Bb7 to cause any trouble. Black has made a mistake with their last move.} 11.Nxc6+ 11...Kc7 {The king slides to c7 to attack the knight.} 12.Nd4 {But we can retreat to d4 and hit the bishop. We've won a clean pawn and Black's position is a mess. Black allowing us to capture on e6 would be horrible for their pawn structure. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 3: Black Plays The 3...g6 System / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/5d423945-0faf-4f28-8d37-65603ff02571/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black connects their pawns. Let's get the other knight to c3 now. We are now going to take a look at a specific variation Black can play.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {It's 3...g6. Black can choose to fianchetto the bishop in basically any Sicilian variation, so this is something we have to cover here as well. Our main antidote to this will be to immediately push d4 and try to get the queen to the center to counteract the bishop. 3...g6 is played just over 5% of the time, in more than 785,000 games.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures. And we will take back with the queen, as mentioned. Black still can't fianchetto.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nf6 {The most natural response is for Black to develop the knight, blocking our queen's vision, and prepare to fianchetto on the next move. This is by far the most common move, played 72% of the time. Let's continue in Anti-Sicilian style, and push e5 now that we see the knight on f6.} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Capturing on e5 only gives us the advantage. We'll trade queens.} 7.Qxd8+ 7...Kxd8 {The idea is that once the king captures... We can take on e5 and threaten a fork. Black has lost their castling rights and they still have to react to our knight.} 8.Nxe5 8...Be6 {They can defend against the threat with the bishop.} 9.Be3 {But we have the momentum and we'll simply continue development. We can castle long with check on the next move and claim a positional as well as an activity advantage. White is much better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 3: Black Plays The 3...g6 System / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/5d423945-0faf-4f28-8d37-65603ff02571/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black connects their pawns. Let's get the other knight to c3 now. We are now going to take a look at a specific variation Black can play.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {It's 3...g6. Black can choose to fianchetto the bishop in basically any Sicilian variation, so this is something we have to cover here as well. Our main antidote to this will be to immediately push d4 and try to get the queen to the center to counteract the bishop. 3...g6 is played just over 5% of the time, in more than 785,000 games.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures. And we will take back with the queen, as mentioned. Black still can't fianchetto.} 5.Qxd4 5...e5 {Going for the quick ...e5 loses the game on the spot. And it's played 10% of the time. We can just sacrifice on e5!} 6.Nxe5 6...Bg7 {An attempt to trick us and pin the knight is for Black to fianchetto now. But it doesn't work.} 7.Qa4+ {Because we can simply slide to a4 with check. If Black blocks with the bishop, we will capture. Otherwise we've just won an important central pawn and Black has no compensation for it.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations / Study 3: Black Plays The 3...g6 System / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fede3b0d-19b9-4c92-b09d-e9d8e110c3a8/chapters/19a193d4-78c7-4cb6-a639-8ea31fb14a7f/studies/5d423945-0faf-4f28-8d37-65603ff02571/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll develop the knight to f3.} 2.Nf3 2...d6 {And Black connects their pawns. Let's get the other knight to c3 now. We are now going to take a look at a specific variation Black can play.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {It's 3...g6. Black can choose to fianchetto the bishop in basically any Sicilian variation, so this is something we have to cover here as well. Our main antidote to this will be to immediately push d4 and try to get the queen to the center to counteract the bishop. 3...g6 is played just over 5% of the time, in more than 785,000 games.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures. And we will take back with the queen, as mentioned. Black still can't fianchetto.} 5.Qxd4 5...e5 {Going for the quick ...e5 loses the game on the spot. And it's played 10% of the time. We can just sacrifice on e5!} 6.Nxe5 6...dxe5 {If Black captures on e5...} 7.Qxe5+ {There's a fork and we'll simply win the rook.} *